{"sounds":{"intro":"Yoruba is a tonal language of Nigeria and Benin. It has three tones: high (á), mid (a, unmarked) and low (à). The tone changes the meaning. Dots under letters (ẹ, ọ, ṣ) are separate letters. Words never change for person or time, and nouns have no gender.","rows":[["ẹ","like \"e\" in \"bed\"","ẹja"],["ọ","like \"o\" in \"more\" or \"thought\"","ọmọ"],["ṣ","like \"sh\" in \"shop\"","ṣé"],["gb","said as one sound, a \"g\" and \"b\" together","gbà"],["kp","a \"k\" and \"p\" said together, like a kiss with a click","kpa"],["p","said as \"kp\" in many words, a sharp \"p\"","pa"],["an / en / in / on / un","a nasal vowel, with the \"n\" not said as a consonant","ń"],["á / à / a","high, low, mid tone on a vowel","bàbá"]],"notes":["The tones are shown here as in standard writing, but people often drop them when they type quickly. Learn them from a speaker, because a wrong tone can change the word.","Yoruba shows respect with the plural \"ẹ\" for one older person. Use \"ẹ\" to a parent, an elder or a stranger and \"o\" to a friend or a child."]},"numbers":[{"id":"0-to-10","title":"0 to 10","items":[["zero","òdo","OH-doh"],["one","ọ̀kan","AW-kahn"],["two","èjì","EH-jee"],["three","ẹ̀ta","EH-tah"],["four","ẹ̀rin","EH-reen"],["five","àrún","AH-roon"],["six","ẹ̀fà","EH-fah"],["seven","èje","EH-jeh"],["eight","ẹ̀jọ","EH-jaw"],["nine","ẹ̀sán","EH-sahn"],["ten","ẹ̀wá","EH-wah"]]},{"id":"11-to-20","title":"11 to 20","items":[["eleven","ọ̀kànlá","AW-kahn-LAH"],["twelve","èjìlá","EH-jee-LAH"],["thirteen","ẹ̀tàlá","EH-tah-LAH"],["fourteen","ẹ̀rinlá","EH-reen-LAH"],["fifteen","ẹ̀ẹ́dógún","EH-eh-DOH-goon"],["sixteen","ẹ̀rìndínlógún","EH-reen-deen-LOH-goon"],["seventeen","ẹ̀tàdínlógún","EH-tah-deen-LOH-goon"],["eighteen","èjìdínlógún","EH-jee-deen-LOH-goon"],["nineteen","ọ̀kàndínlógún","AW-kahn-deen-LOH-goon"],["twenty","ogún","OH-goon"]]},{"id":"tens-and-big-numbers","title":"Tens and big numbers","items":[["twenty-one","ọ̀kànlélógún","AW-kahn-LEH-loh-goon"],["thirty",